;;; (sigil coin node rules) — SigilCoin header acceptance and fork choice
;;;
;;; This is the seam between SigilCoin's consensus package and
;;; `sigil-bitcoin-node`. The library never interprets what a rule means: it
;;; calls `accept?` to admit a header, `chain-work` to rank a chain, and
;;; `better-chain?` to choose a tip. SigilCoin supplies all three here and
;;; attaches the record to its chain config, so the node runs SigilCoin's
;;; rules without the Bitcoin library learning anything about program golf.
;;;
;;; No hash proof-of-work is involved anywhere in this module. A header is
;;; never hashed to compare against a target; the header hash is used only as
;;; the tie-break of last resort, exactly as `coin-solution-compare` does.
;;;
;;; ============================================================
;;; THE RANKING KEY
;;; ============================================================
;;;
;;; `chain-work` must return a single integer, because that is what the
;;; library persists and hands back in a `chain-candidate`. SigilCoin needs
;;; two facts in it: how well the whole chain has been golfed, and how short
;;; the TIP's own solution is. They are packed as two digits in base
;;; `coin-rank-base`:
;;;
;;;   work = accumulated * coin-rank-base + tip-saving
;;;
;;; where `saving` for one block is
;;;
;;;   saving = (coin-max-solution-bytes + 1) - solution-length
;;;
;;; so a 1-byte solution scores 256, a 256-byte solution scores 1, and
;;; shorter is always larger. `accumulated` is the sum of the savings of
;;; every block from genesis to the tip; `tip-saving` is the last block's
;;; saving alone. `coin-rank-base` is 258: the low digit ranges over 0..256,
;;; which needs a base of at least 257, and 258 is that with one spare
;;; value. The two digits therefore never collide and both are recoverable:
;;; `coin-rank-accumulated` and `coin-rank-tip-saving` read them back.
;;;
;;; A saving of 0 is reserved for a header that does not carry a legal
;;; solution length at all, which `accept?` refuses to store.
;;;
;;; ============================================================
;;; WHY THE HEADER COMMITS TO THE SOLUTION LENGTH
;;; ============================================================
;;;
;;; Fork choice has to be decidable from HEADERS ALONE. That is not a
;;; preference, it is what makes headers-first sync possible: a node ranks
;;; competing chains to decide which bodies are worth downloading, so a rank
;;; that needs the body is circular. Bitcoin has the property for free —
;;; its work is `bits` plus the hash, both in the header.
;;;
;;; SigilCoin's rank is a solution length, and the solution lives in the
;;; coinbase. So the header commits to its length, in the 32-bit field
;;; Bitcoin calls `nonce` and SigilCoin has no other use for; there is no
;;; hash to grind, so the field was otherwise dead. `accept?` bounds it to
;;; 1..`coin-max-solution-bytes` and `coin-header-solution-length` reads it.
;;;
;;; The commitment is a CLAIM, and body validation is where it is checked:
;;; `coin-connect-block` rejects a block whose coinbase solution is not
;;; exactly as long as its header said. A lie is therefore not a cheap way
;;; to outrank an honest chain — it is an invalid block, and the node marks
;;; the header and its whole descendant branch invalid, permanently.
;;;
;;; WHAT THIS BUYS. Provisional and settled ranks are the same number, so a
;;; shorter sibling outranks a longer incumbent the moment its HEADER
;;; arrives, and the node then pulls its body and validates it. Without the
;;; commitment the rank could only come from a validated body, and a node
;;; only validates bodies on its current best chain: a shorter sibling would
;;; be unrankable until validated and unvalidatable until ranked, and the
;;; reorganisation `coin-replaces-tip?` requires could never happen.
;;;
;;; `db-settle-header-work` still runs when a body validates and still
;;; rewrites the same number, exactly as it does for Bitcoin.
;;;
;;; HOW IT COMPOSES ACROSS HEIGHTS. No single accumulated number can make
;;; "taller wins" and "shorter wins" compose by magnitude alone: one extra
;;; block adds at most 256 to the sum, so a height-2 chain of long solutions
;;; would lose to a height-1 chain of one very short one at any fixed
;;; weighting. Height therefore outranks the packed number in
;;; `better-chain?`, and the packed number only ever decides a comparison
;;; BETWEEN SIBLINGS AT THE SAME HEIGHT — which is precisely the comparison
;;; `coin-solution-compare` defines. What the packing still guarantees is
;;; that the number orders chains sensibly on its own terms: it is strictly
;;; increasing along any chain, because every block adds at least 1 to the
;;; high digit while the low digit can never carry more than 256, which is
;;; less than one unit of the high digit. A chain therefore never ranks below
;;; one of its own ancestors, and the high digit stays a meaningful "total
;;; golf quality" for status output.
;;;
;;; GENESIS RANKS LIKE EVERY OTHER BLOCK. The library seeds its header table
;;; with `(chain-work genesis #f 0)` and never revisits it, because genesis
;;; has no body to download and no peer to download it from. The header
;;; commitment is what makes that one-shot value the true one: genesis
;;; contributes what its own solution is worth, not a placeholder every
;;; later chain would then inherit.
;;;
;;; ============================================================
;;; HEADER ACCEPTANCE
;;; ============================================================
;;;
;;; `accept?` is `coin-validate-header-context` (linkage, spacing floor,
;;; median time past, future drift) plus the header fields SigilCoin
;;; constrains: `version`, `bits` and the solution-length commitment.
;;;
;;; `bits` is NOT difficulty here and is never retargeted; it is pinned to
;;; `coin-header-bits` so no miner can vary it. Pinning it removes a free
;;; field, not the game: the coinbase graffiti is still 400 free bytes and
;;; the hash tie-break is still grindable through it.
;;;
;;; `version` is pinned too, and something does read it. Bitcoin's block
;;; connector enforces the buried deployment version floors, and SigilCoin
;;; activates every one of them at height 0, so `version` is pinned to the
;;; value those floors require rather than to 1. See `coin-header-version`.
;;;
;;; The spacing floor is chain policy, so the rules record is built per
;;; chain: `(coin-header-rules min-block-spacing)`.

(define-library (sigil coin node rules)
  (import (sigil math)
          (sigil time)
          (sigil bitcoin consensus)
          (sigil coin consensus))
  (export
    coin-header-version
    coin-header-bits
    coin-header-solution-length
    coin-header-solution-length-ok?
    coin-rank-base
    coin-solution-saving
    coin-rank-encode
    coin-rank-accumulated
    coin-rank-tip-saving
    coin-block-solution-length
    coin-chain-work
    coin-better-chain?
    coin-accept-header?
    coin-header-rules)

  (begin

    ;; ============================================================
    ;; Frozen header fields
    ;; ============================================================

    ;;; The only block header version SigilCoin accepts.
    ;;;
    ;;; Four, not one, and it is not a free choice. SigilCoin activates
    ;;; BIP34, BIP66 and BIP65 at height 0, and Bitcoin's block connector
    ;;; reads those activation heights as version floors: an active BIP34
    ;;; requires version 2 or newer, BIP66 version 3, BIP65 version 4. A
    ;;; version-1 SigilCoin block is therefore rejected by the connector at
    ;;; every height, which is not a rule SigilCoin gets to opt out of while
    ;;; also claiming those deployments are on. Four is what "every buried
    ;;; deployment is active from genesis" spells in a block header.
    ;;;
    ;;; A new chain has no version-rolled soft fork ahead of it, so this stays
    ;;; a constant rather than becoming a signalling field.
    (define coin-header-version 4)

    ;;; The only `bits` value SigilCoin accepts.
    ;;;
    ;;; This is not a difficulty. It is the compact encoding of the largest
    ;;; representable target, so any Bitcoin-derived helper that insists on
    ;;; reading it computes "the whole hash space", i.e. no work at all.
    (define coin-header-bits #x207fffff)

    ;;; The solution length a header commits to, read out of the 32-bit
    ;;; field Bitcoin calls `nonce`.
    ;;;
    ;;; SigilCoin has no hash to grind, so the field carries the one number
    ;;; fork choice needs and the header would not otherwise have: how many
    ;;; bytes of puzzle-lang source this block's coinbase claims. See the
    ;;; module header for why the rank has to be readable from the header.
    ;;;
    ;;; The value is a claim. `coin-connect-block` rejects the block if the
    ;;; coinbase disagrees with it.
    ;;;
    ;;; ```scheme
    ;;; (coin-header-solution-length header) ; => 49
    ;;; ```
    (define (coin-header-solution-length header)
      (: block-header? -> integer?)
      (block-header-nonce header))

    ;;; Whether `length` is a solution length a header may commit to.
    ;;;
    ;;; The bounds are the coinbase payload's own: a solution is at least one
    ;;; byte and at most `coin-max-solution-bytes`, so a header claiming
    ;;; anything else describes a block that could never decode.
    ;;;
    ;;; ```scheme
    ;;; (coin-header-solution-length-ok? 0)   ; => #f
    ;;; (coin-header-solution-length-ok? 49)  ; => #t
    ;;; ```
    (define (coin-header-solution-length-ok? length)
      (: any? -> boolean?)
      (and (number? length) (exact? length) (integer? length)
           (>= length 1)
           (<= length coin-max-solution-bytes)))

    ;; ============================================================
    ;; The ranking key
    ;; ============================================================

    ;;; Base of the two-digit ranking key: two more than the largest saving.
    ;;;
    ;;; The low digit is a saving, which ranges over 0..`coin-max-solution-bytes`
    ;;; — 257 distinct values, so 257 is the smallest base that keeps the two
    ;;; digits from overlapping. This is 258, one above that floor.
    (define coin-rank-base (+ coin-max-solution-bytes 2))

    ;;; Score of a solution of `solution-length` bytes: shorter is larger.
    ;;;
    ;;; A length outside 1..`coin-max-solution-bytes` scores 0, the reserved
    ;;; "not a legal length" value, because no such solution can appear in a
    ;;; valid block. `accept?` refuses to store a header claiming one, so a
    ;;; stored chain never carries a 0.
    ;;;
    ;;; ```scheme
    ;;; (coin-solution-saving 1)   ; => 256
    ;;; (coin-solution-saving 256) ; => 1
    ;;; (coin-solution-saving 0)   ; => 0
    ;;; ```
    (define (coin-solution-saving solution-length)
      (: any? -> integer?)
      (if (coin-header-solution-length-ok? solution-length)
          (- (+ coin-max-solution-bytes 1) solution-length)
          0))

    ;;; Pack an accumulated total and a tip saving into the ranking key.
    ;;;
    ;;; ```scheme
    ;;; (coin-rank-encode 3 256) ; => 3 * coin-rank-base + 256
    ;;; ```
    (define (coin-rank-encode accumulated tip-saving)
      (: integer? integer? -> integer?)
      (+ (* accumulated coin-rank-base) tip-saving))

    ;;; The accumulated golf total inside a ranking key.
    ;;;
    ;;; ```scheme
    ;;; (coin-rank-accumulated (coin-rank-encode 3 256)) ; => 3
    ;;; ```
    (define (coin-rank-accumulated work)
      (: integer? -> integer?)
      (quotient work coin-rank-base))

    ;;; The tip's own saving inside a ranking key.
    ;;;
    ;;; ```scheme
    ;;; (coin-rank-tip-saving (coin-rank-encode 3 256)) ; => 256
    ;;; ```
    (define (coin-rank-tip-saving work)
      (: integer? -> integer?)
      (remainder work coin-rank-base))

    ;;; Length of a block's claimed solution, or `#f` when the block has no
    ;;; decodable SigilCoin coinbase payload.
    ;;;
    ;;; Total: a hostile or malformed body yields `#f` rather than a raise,
    ;;; so ranking a block never has to be wrapped in a handler. Such a block
    ;;; cannot pass solution verification either, so it never becomes a tip.
    ;;;
    ;;; ```scheme
    ;;; (coin-block-solution-length 42) ; => #f
    ;;; ```
    (define (coin-block-solution-length b)
      (: any? -> any?)
      (let ((decoded (coin-block-coinbase-payload b)))
        (and (coin-ok? decoded)
             (bytevector-length
               (coin-coinbase-payload-solution (coin-result-value decoded))))))

    ;;; Accumulated ranking key of the chain ending at `header`.
    ;;;
    ;;; `previous-work` is the parent's key. `b` is the validated block body
    ;;; when the node has one and `#f` during headers-first sync; it is
    ;;; accepted to satisfy the seam and deliberately IGNORED, because the
    ;;; length the rank is built from is committed in the header. That is
    ;;; what makes the provisional value at header ingest and the settled
    ;;; value after body validation the same number, and it is what lets a
    ;;; shorter sibling outrank a longer incumbent before anyone has its
    ;;; body. `coin-connect-block` is where the header's claim is checked
    ;;; against the body it summarises.
    ;;;
    ;;; ```scheme
    ;;; (coin-rank-tip-saving (coin-chain-work header #f 0))
    ;;; ; => the saving of the length the header commits to
    ;;; ```
    (define (coin-chain-work header b previous-work)
      (: any? any? integer? -> integer?)
      (let* ((saving (if (block-header? header)
                         (coin-solution-saving
                           (coin-header-solution-length header))
                         0))
             ;; A header `accept?` would refuse still has to rank as
             ;; something; one is the least any legal block contributes.
             (contribution (if (= 0 saving) 1 saving)))
        (coin-rank-encode (+ (coin-rank-accumulated previous-work)
                             contribution)
                          saving)))

    ;;; Whether `candidate` strictly outranks `incumbent` as the chain tip.
    ;;;
    ;;; The order is: a live candidate beats a settled one; then the taller
    ;;; chain; then the shorter tip solution; then the lower internal block
    ;;; hash. That is `coin-replaces-tip?` and `coin-solution-compare`
    ;;; expressed over the candidates the library hands out.
    ;;;
    ;;; SETTLED MEANS OUTGROWN, NOT DESCENDED FROM. `extended?` is set by
    ;;; `sigil-bitcoin`'s `header-row->chain-candidate` as `height <` the
    ;;; tallest stored header height, so a candidate is settled as soon as
    ;;; ANY taller header is stored, on any branch — not only when a child of
    ;;; that candidate exists. Stored heights are contiguous, so the two agree
    ;;; whenever one branch is doing the growing; they part company across
    ;;; branches, where a taller sibling branch settles this height too. The
    ;;; effect is the same one the rule wants: heights the chain has already
    ;;; grown past do not get relitigated by a late better sibling.
    ;;;
    ;;; "Strictly" is literal: this is a strict total order, so it is
    ;;; irreflexive (a candidate never outranks itself, the hash tie-break
    ;;; comparing equal) and antisymmetric (at most one direction is `#t`).
    ;;; That is why settledness is compared as a RANK — live above settled —
    ;;; rather than as an early `#f` on any extended candidate: two extended
    ;;; candidates are equally settled, so they fall through to the ordinary
    ;;; height/solution/hash comparison instead of both answering `#f`.
    ;;; `best-row` never depends on that case today, because the tallest
    ;;; stored height is never extended and so always ends as the incumbent,
    ;;; but a contract that only holds for the inputs one caller happens to
    ;;; produce is not a contract.
    ;;;
    ;;; ```scheme
    ;;; (coin-better-chain? taller shorter-but-lower) ; => #t
    ;;; ```
    (define (coin-better-chain? candidate incumbent)
      (: chain-candidate? chain-candidate? -> boolean?)
      (let ((candidate-extended? (chain-candidate-extended? candidate))
            (incumbent-extended? (chain-candidate-extended? incumbent))
            (candidate-height (chain-candidate-height candidate))
            (incumbent-height (chain-candidate-height incumbent)))
        (cond
          ;; A height some taller header has already outgrown is settled: no
          ;; sibling replaces a live tip however short its solution.
          ((and candidate-extended? (not incumbent-extended?)) #f)
          ;; A settled incumbent is one the chain has already moved past, so
          ;; any still-extendable candidate takes over. This is what walks
          ;; the tip forward off genesis.
          ((and incumbent-extended? (not candidate-extended?)) #t)
          ;; Equally settled, so settledness decides nothing: rank them the
          ;; ordinary way.
          ((> candidate-height incumbent-height) #t)
          ((< candidate-height incumbent-height) #f)
          ;; Same height: the shorter solution wins. The header commits to
          ;; the length, so this comparison is decidable the moment both
          ;; headers are stored, before either body is known.
          ((> (coin-rank-tip-saving (chain-candidate-work candidate))
              (coin-rank-tip-saving (chain-candidate-work incumbent)))
           #t)
          ((< (coin-rank-tip-saving (chain-candidate-work candidate))
              (coin-rank-tip-saving (chain-candidate-work incumbent)))
           #f)
          (else
            (< (coin-hash-compare (chain-candidate-hash candidate)
                                  (chain-candidate-hash incumbent))
               0)))))

    ;; ============================================================
    ;; Header acceptance
    ;; ============================================================

    (define (current-unix-second)
      (exact (floor (current-second))))

    ;;; Whether `header` may extend `previous-headers` under `min-spacing`.
    ;;;
    ;;; `params` is accepted and ignored: SigilCoin has no difficulty to read
    ;;; out of it, and the fields it does carry are activation heights the
    ;;; header cannot violate. `previous-headers` runs oldest to newest and
    ;;; is empty only for genesis.
    ;;;
    ;;; ```scheme
    ;;; (coin-accept-header? params (list genesis) header 72000)
    ;;; ```
    (define (coin-accept-header? params previous-headers header min-spacing)
      (: any? list? any? integer? -> boolean?)
      (and (block-header? header)
           (= coin-header-version (block-header-version header))
           (= coin-header-bits (block-header-bits header))
           ;; The claim only has to be a legal length here. Whether it is a
           ;; TRUE claim is a body rule, checked by `coin-connect-block`.
           (coin-header-solution-length-ok?
             (coin-header-solution-length header))
           (coin-ok? (coin-validate-header-context previous-headers
                                                   header
                                                   (current-unix-second)
                                                   min-spacing))))

    ;;; Build the `header-rules` record for a chain with `min-spacing`.
    ;;;
    ;;; The spacing floor is the only chain-varying policy: mainnet passes
    ;;; `coin-min-block-spacing`, a local test chain passes something a test
    ;;; can wait out. Everything else is chain-independent consensus.
    ;;;
    ;;; ```scheme
    ;;; (header-rules? (coin-header-rules coin-min-block-spacing)) ; => #t
    ;;; ```
    (define (coin-header-rules min-spacing)
      (: integer? -> header-rules?)
      (if (not (and (number? min-spacing) (exact? min-spacing)
                    (integer? min-spacing) (>= min-spacing 0)))
          (error "coin-header-rules: expected a spacing floor in seconds"))
      (header-rules
        accept?: (lambda (params previous-headers header)
                   (coin-accept-header? params
                                        previous-headers
                                        header
                                        min-spacing))
        chain-work: coin-chain-work
        better-chain?: coin-better-chain?))))
